fn simd_operations(input1: &[u16], input2: &[u16]) -> ([u16; 8], [u16; 8]) {
    let mut result1 = [0u16; 8];
    let mut result2 = [0u16; 8];

    let m3 = SPLIT_M3 as u32;
    let m4 = SPLIT_M4 as u32;

    for i in 0..8 {
        let val1 = input1[i] as u32;
        let val2 = input2[i] as u32;

        // Perform SIMD-like operations
        result1[i] = ((val1 * m3) >> 16) as u16; // High 16 bits of (val1 * m3)
        result2[i] = ((val1 * m4) & 0xFFFF) as u16; // Low 16 bits of (val1 * m4)

        result1[i + 1] = ((val2 * m4) & 0xFFFF) as u16; // Low 16 bits of (val2 * m4)
        result2[i + 1] = ((val2 * m3) >> 16) as u16; // High 16 bits of (val2 * m3)
    }

    (result1, result2)
}
```

In the solution, we iterate through the input arrays and perform the specified SIMD-like operations on each element. The results are stored in separate arrays and returned as a tuple. The operations involve multiplication with constants and extracting the high and low 16 bits of the results, simulating the SIMD operations described in the code snippet.

