fn longest_substring(s: &str) -> String {
    let mut max_len = 0;
    let mut start = 0;
    let mut result = String::new();
    let mut char_indices = HashMap::new();

    for (i, c) in s.chars().enumerate() {
        if let Some(prev_index) = char_indices.get(&c) {
            start = start.max(*prev_index + 1);
        }
        if i - start + 1 > max_len {
            max_len = i - start + 1;
            result = s[start..=i].to_string();
        }
        char_indices.insert(c, i);
    }

    result
}
```

The `longest_substring` function iterates through the input string `s` using the `chars` method to handle Unicode characters properly. It uses a HashMap `char_indices` to store the most recent index of each character encountered. The `start` variable keeps track of the start index of the current substring without repeating characters. If a repeating character is encountered, the `start` index is updated to the next index after the previous occurrence of the character. The function then calculates the length of the current substring and updates the result if a longer substring is found. Finally, it returns the longest substring without repeating characters.

