        self.edges.insert(new_edge);
        new_edge.data().is_constraint_edge()
    }

    fn intersects_existing_constraint(&self, new_edge: &Edge) -> bool {
        for existing_edge in &self.edges {
            if new_edge.intersects(existing_edge) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        false
    }
}
```

In the solution, we define the `Point` and `Edge` structs and the `ConstraintGraph` struct to represent the constraint graph. The `insert_constraint` method creates a new edge between the given points and checks if it intersects with any existing constraint edge. If it does, the method panics; otherwise, the new edge is added to the graph, and the method returns `true` if it represents a constraint edge. The `intersects_existing_constraint` method checks if the new edge intersects with any existing constraint edge in the graph.

